Synthesis
  1. The n=14 dead end sharpened: the coordinate-6 half-turn is not just
     the best binary obstruction, it is the unique global minimum of the
     full normalized 2-torsion cube.  Adding any second defect to it
     immediately exposes more cells.
  2. The n=15 replacement obstruction is not binary; its natural small
     probes are the CRT subgroups of Z/15Z.  Exact small-support scans and
     local search still leave positive missed-cell margins.
  3. Disproof pressure keeps leaking: gated and two-gate interval-cover
     candidates retain explicit positive complement gaps.
  4. New proof idea: treat n=14 as a 2-torsion/chirality boundary and n=15
     as a CRT-subgroup leakage problem.  A unified lemma should say every
     non-scalar quotient defect exposes either a torsion stencil or a CRT
     subgroup leak before it can become an open cover.
